    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pick\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.10.x\n\n### Description\n\nThis may be intentional behavior, but it confused me for a bit.\n\nWhen one of the built-in pickers is called through the registry, it only accepts the first picker argument (local_opts). I had assumed that a picker called through the registry would accept the same arguments as a picker called directly.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n1. Have mini.pick and mini.extra installed and configured\n2. Open a file with some reported diagnostics\n3. Open the diagnostic picker with\n   ```\n   :lua MiniExtra.pickers.diagnostic({}, { source = { preview = function() print(\"previewing\") end } })\n   ```\n4. Hit Tab to preview a diagnostic; note that the preview window is empty and \"previewing\" shows up in the message area\n5. Close the picker\n6. Open the diagnostic picker with\n   ```\n   :lua MiniPick.registry.diagnostic({}, { source = { preview = function() print(\"previewing\") end } })\n   ```\n7. Hit Tab to preview a diagnostic; note that the default previewer is used and \"previewing\" is not printed in the message area",
